<h2>Security</h2>
<p>
    At a shallow level, software security is about doing what everyone says to do.
At a deeper level, software security is about understanding the systems you're using.

Security directly opposes usability.
</p>

<h2>Test-Driven Development</h2>
<p>
    TDD only works when the problem has already been solved *and* the API has already been chosen.
Most of the time it is counterproductive. But in some cases, such as when implementing an RFC spec, it may be helpful.
Using it 100% of the time is a terrible idea.

I used TDD when writing a [WebSocket server in Go](), 
and it was extremely productive because the [test suite](https://github.com/crossbario/autobahn-testsuite) already existed.
</p>

<h2>Testing</h2>
<p>
    Automated testing is an important part of writing software that works.
However, it has a cost, and some programs are harder to test than others.
Rather than declaring that all code should have unit tests, I prefer to see it
in terms of a cost/benefit analysis.
Code with objective results that is easy to test should always have tests.
Code with subjective results, or that has significant barriers to testing, may not always be worth it to test.

100% test coverage does not guarantee that the tests are any good.
I prefer fewer high-quality tests to 100% coverage with low-quality tests.
A test suite that has 100% coverage, but only tests 10% of the edge cases, is not a good test suite.

Tests should be focused on module APIs. Module internals don't *need* tests, but sometimes
it is easier to debug the internals when there are tests.
However, the cost of writing tests for internals is that the code gets harder to change,
which breaks the promise that unit tests are supposed to make refactoring easier.
Unit tests on internals should therefore be considered throwaway.

Mocks should only be used when absolutely necessary.
When possible, broaden the scope of the test so that the thing that needs to be mocked is included in the test.
I have seen tests

I don't find the unit/integration/e2e test distinction useful.
</p>

